```csharp
using System.Net;
using DotNetEnv;
using InfluxDB3.Client;
using InfluxDB3.Client.Write;

Env.Load();
string Require(string name) =>
    Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(name)
    ?? throw new InvalidOperationException($"Missing env var {name}");

var database = Require("INFLUXDB_DATABASE");
using var client = new InfluxDBClient(
    host: Require("INFLUXDB_HOST"),
    token: Require("INFLUXDB_TOKEN"),
    database: database);

// InfluxDB 3 silently auto-creates databases on write. A typo like "senor_data"
// would "succeed" and put your data in the wrong place. Fail fast instead.
try
{
    await foreach (var _ in client.Query("SHOW TABLES")) break;
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    Console.Error.WriteLine($"Database '{database}' not reachable or doesn't exist: {ex.Message}");
    return 1;
}

const int TotalPoints   = 1_000_000;
const int BatchSize     = 10_000;    // docs: up to 10,000 lines or 10 MB per request
const int MaxInFlight   = 4;         // concurrent requests; start small
const int MaxAttempts   = 5;
const int Hosts         = 100;       // 100 hosts × 10,000 seconds = 1M distinct points

var start = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.AddSeconds(-TotalPoints / Hosts).ToUnixTimeSeconds();

// Generate batches lazily so all 1M PointData objects are never in memory at once.
IEnumerable<List<PointData>> Batches()
{
    var batch = new List<PointData>(BatchSize);
    for (int i = 0; i < TotalPoints; i++)
    {
        batch.Add(PointData.Measurement("sensor")
            .SetTag("host", $"server{i % Hosts:D3}")
            .SetField("temperature", 20 + Random.Shared.NextDouble() * 10)
            .SetField("humidity", 40 + Random.Shared.NextDouble() * 20)
            // Always write the event's own timestamp. Points with the same
            // table + tags + timestamp merge, so each host gets one point per second.
            .SetTimestamp(start + i / Hosts, WritePrecision.S));

        if (batch.Count == BatchSize)
        {
            yield return batch;
            batch = new List<PointData>(BatchSize);
        }
    }
    if (batch.Count > 0) yield return batch;
}

static bool IsRetriable(Exception ex) => ex switch
{
    InfluxDBApiException api => api.StatusCode == HttpStatusCode.TooManyRequests
                                || (int)api.StatusCode >= 500,
    HttpRequestException     => true,   // no response: connection refused/reset
    TaskCanceledException    => true,   // timeout
    _                        => false,  // 400/401/403/404/413: fix, don't retry
};

async Task WriteWithRetry(List<PointData> batch, int batchNo)
{
    for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++)
    {
        try
        {
            await client.WritePointsAsync(batch, precision: WritePrecision.S);
            return;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (IsRetriable(ex) && attempt < MaxAttempts)
        {
            // Exponential backoff with full jitter
            var delayMs = Random.Shared.Next(0, (int)Math.Min(30_000, 250 * Math.Pow(2, attempt)));
            Console.Error.WriteLine($"Batch {batchNo} attempt {attempt} failed ({ex.Message}); retrying in {delayMs} ms");
            await Task.Delay(delayMs);
        }
    }
}

var sw = System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch.StartNew();
var batchNo = 0;
await Parallel.ForEachAsync(
    Batches().Select(b => (b, n: Interlocked.Increment(ref batchNo))),
    new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = MaxInFlight },
    async (item, _) => await WriteWithRetry(item.b, item.n));

Console.WriteLine($"Wrote {TotalPoints:N0} points in {sw.Elapsed.TotalSeconds:F1}s " +
                  $"({TotalPoints / sw.Elapsed.TotalSeconds:N0} pts/s)");
return 0;
```

## Why it's written this way

- **10,000-point batches** means 100 requests in total, not 1M. Writing one point per request is the most common reason bulk loads are slow, and it can also run out of HTTP connections.
- **Lazy batch generation plus a limit on concurrent requests** keeps memory use flat while several requests run at once. `MaxInFlight = 4` is a cautious starting point.
- **Every point has its own timestamp.** If you leave it out, the server stamps the time it received the point, so a retried batch would show up as duplicate points. With a fixed timestamp, a retry merges into the original point. When two points merge, there's no guarantee which field value is kept.
- **Second precision with 100 hosts** means no two points share the same tags and timestamp. If one series can have more than one point per second, switch to `WritePrecision.Ms` or a finer precision. Otherwise points will merge and you'll appear to lose data.
- **What gets retried:** 429, 5xx and network failures are retried with backoff and jitter. 400, 401, 403, 404 and 413 fail right away because you need to fix the data, token or batch size first. If a batch fails for good, `Parallel.ForEachAsync` stops the run and passes on the exception.
- **By default, the client writes through `/api/v2/write`** (client 1.9.0+). There, one bad line rejects the whole batch. If you want partial writes or `NoSync`, set the `UseV2Api` write option to `false` to use `/api/v3/write_lp`. In that mode a 400 means the valid lines were already stored. The response body lists the rejected lines by `line_number`, so resend only those after fixing them, not the whole batch.

## Before you rely on it

- **Untested:** I haven't compiled or run this code.
- **Check against your client version:** `InfluxDBApiException.StatusCode` and the `precision:` parameter on `WritePointsAsync` are how I understand recent `InfluxDB3.Client` releases. Confirm them in the README for the version you install.
- **Tag values from untrusted input:** strip or reject `\n` and `\r` first. A newline in line protocol starts a new record, and the point builder can't escape it.
- **Tuning:** this skill doesn't cover tuning batch size or concurrency for a specific workload. See https://docs.influxdata.com/influxdb3/core/write-data/best-practices/optimize-writes/. The one-line version: measure throughput as you raise `MaxInFlight`, and stop when it levels off or you start getting 429s or 5xx errors.

Below is a complete example using the official `InfluxDB3.Client` package. It assumes InfluxDB 3 Core or Enterprise (or InfluxDB 3 Cloud).

### How it works

- **10,000 points per request.** 1M points becomes 100 requests. 10,000 lines (or 10 MB) is the recommended maximum per write.
- **Points are built in batches as they're sent.** You never hold 1M `PointData` objects in memory.
- **Up to 4 requests run at once,** limited by a `SemaphoreSlim`. More parallel requests than that tends to move the bottleneck to the server.
- **Every point gets its own timestamp.** Without one, a retried batch would be stored as new duplicate points. With one, a retry merges into the points already written.
- **Second precision is safe here** because each series (`sensor_id`) gets at most one point per second. Points with the same table, tags and timestamp merge, and there's no guaranteed winner for which field value is kept. If a series can get more than one point per second, use finer precision.

### Setup (do these first)

1. **Check the server is up:** `curl -i "$INFLUXDB_HOST/ping" -H "Authorization: Bearer $INFLUXDB_TOKEN"` should return 200 with an `x-influxdb-build` header.
2. **Create an admin token** if you don't have one: `influxdb3 create token --admin`. The secret is shown only once, so save it as `INFLUXDB_ADMIN_TOKEN`.
3. **Create the database:** `influxdb3 create database sensor_data --token "$INFLUXDB_ADMIN_TOKEN"`
4. **Create a separate token for the app.** On Enterprise or InfluxDB 3 Cloud, use a resource token limited to the database (`db:sensor_data:read,write`). On Core, which only has admin tokens, create a named admin token just for this app.
5. **Set env vars in `.env`, and add `.env` to `.gitignore` first:**
   ```
   INFLUXDB_HOST=http://localhost:8181
   INFLUXDB_TOKEN=<app token>
   INFLUXDB_DATABASE=sensor_data
   ```
6. **Install the packages:**
   ```bash
   dotnet add package InfluxDB3.Client
   dotnet add package DotNetEnv
   ```

### Code

```csharp
using System.Net;
using DotNetEnv;
using InfluxDB3.Client;
using InfluxDB3.Client.Write;

Env.Load();

string Require(string name) =>
    Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(name)
    ?? throw new InvalidOperationException($"Missing env var {name}");

using var client = new InfluxDBClient(
    host: Require("INFLUXDB_HOST"),
    token: Require("INFLUXDB_TOKEN"),
    database: Require("INFLUXDB_DATABASE"));

// InfluxDB 3 silently creates a database on first write, so a typo like
// "senor_data" would "succeed" into the wrong database. Queries don't
// auto-create, so a quick query fails fast if the name is wrong.
try
{
    await foreach (var _ in client.Query("SELECT 1")) { }
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    Console.Error.WriteLine(
        $"Database '{Require("INFLUXDB_DATABASE")}' isn't reachable or doesn't exist: {ex.Message}");
    return 1;
}

const int TotalPoints = 1_000_000;
const int BatchSize   = 10_000;
const int Sensors     = 1_000;          // 1,000 sensors x 1,000 seconds = 1M unique points
const int MaxParallel = 4;

var start = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeSeconds() - TotalPoints / Sensors;
var gate = new SemaphoreSlim(MaxParallel);
var tasks = new List<Task>();
var sw = System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch.StartNew();

for (int offset = 0; offset < TotalPoints; offset += BatchSize)
{
    await gate.WaitAsync();
    int batchStart = offset;
    tasks.Add(Task.Run(async () =>
    {
        try
        {
            var batch = BuildBatch(batchStart, Math.Min(BatchSize, TotalPoints - batchStart));
            await WriteWithRetryAsync(batch);
        }
        finally { gate.Release(); }
    }));
}

await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
Console.WriteLine($"Wrote {TotalPoints:N0} points in {sw.Elapsed.TotalSeconds:F1}s");
return 0;

List<PointData> BuildBatch(int from, int count)
{
    var list = new List<PointData>(count);
    var rng = Random.Shared;
    for (int i = from; i < from + count; i++)
    {
        int sensor = i % Sensors;
        long ts = start + i / Sensors;   // one point per sensor per second
        list.Add(PointData.Measurement("sensor_reading")
            .SetTag("sensor_id", $"sensor_{sensor:D4}")
            .SetTag("region", sensor % 2 == 0 ? "us_west" : "us_east")
            .SetField("temperature", 20 + rng.NextDouble() * 10)
            .SetField("humidity", 40 + rng.NextDouble() * 20)
            .SetTimestamp(ts, WritePrecision.S));
    }
    return list;
}

async Task WriteWithRetryAsync(List<PointData> batch)
{
    const int MaxAttempts = 5;
    for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++)
    {
        try
        {
            await client.WritePointsAsync(batch, precision: WritePrecision.S);
            return;
        }
        catch (InfluxDBApiException ex) when (IsRetryable(ex.StatusCode) && attempt < MaxAttempts)
        {
            await Backoff(attempt);
        }
        catch (HttpRequestException) when (attempt < MaxAttempts) // no response
        {
            await Backoff(attempt);
        }
        // 400/401/403/404/413 (and exhausted retries) propagate to the caller.
    }
}

static bool IsRetryable(HttpStatusCode code) =>
    code == (HttpStatusCode)429 || (int)code >= 500;

static Task Backoff(int attempt)
{
    var baseMs = 200 * Math.Pow(2, attempt - 1);         // 200, 400, 800, 1600 ms
    var jitter = Random.Shared.NextDouble() * baseMs;    // full jitter
    return Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(baseMs / 2 + jitter));
}
```

### Things to know

- **Which endpoint it uses.** Recent versions of the client (1.9.0+) write through `/api/v2/write`. On that endpoint, one bad line rejects the whole batch: fix it and resend the whole batch.
  - To use `/api/v3/write_lp` instead, set the `UseV2Api` write option to `false`. That endpoint stores the valid lines and returns a 400 listing the rejected ones by `line_number`. It also supports `NoSync`.
  - With `/api/v3/write_lp`, resend only the corrected rejected lines, not the whole batch.
- **Retries:** a retry may succeed, but repeating a write isn't guaranteed to be harmless. The timestamps are what keep retries from creating duplicate points.
- **Real input data:** if tag or field values come from outside your app, strip or reject `\n` and `\r` first. Line protocol can't escape them, so a newline in a value starts a forged extra record. `PointData` escapes the other special characters for you.
- **Tuning:** this covers basic batching. Finding the best batch size and parallelism for your workload is performance tuning, which isn't covered here; see the InfluxDB 3 docs. As a starting point, try `MaxParallel` between 2 and 8 and measure.
- **Exact API names:** I wrote `InfluxDBApiException.StatusCode` and the `precision:` argument from memory, not from the package docs. If they don't compile on your version, check https://github.com/InfluxCommunity/influxdb3-csharp.

Here's a working pattern for writing 1M points to InfluxDB 3 Core or Enterprise with the official `InfluxDB3.Client` package. It splits the points into 100 batches of 10,000 (the recommended maximum per request), builds each batch only when it's needed so all 1M points are never in memory at once, sends 4 batches at a time, and retries only the errors worth retrying.

## Setup (do these before running)

1. **The server is reachable:** `curl -i "$INFLUXDB_HOST/ping" -H "Authorization: Bearer $INFLUXDB_TOKEN"` should return 200 with an `x-influxdb-build` header. A 401 still means the server is up.
2. **An admin token exists:** `influxdb3 create token --admin`. It prints the secret only once, so save it right away as `INFLUXDB_ADMIN_TOKEN` (not in code).
3. **The database exists:** `influxdb3 create database sensor_data --token "$INFLUXDB_ADMIN_TOKEN"`.
4. **The app has its own token:**
   - Enterprise or InfluxDB 3 Cloud: a resource token scoped to `db:sensor_data:read,write`.
   - Core: a separate named admin token (Core has no scoped tokens).
5. **Env vars are set:** `INFLUXDB_HOST`, `INFLUXDB_TOKEN`, `INFLUXDB_DATABASE`. If you use a `.env` file, **add `.env` to `.gitignore` first**, because one commit of it publishes the token.

```bash
dotnet add package InfluxDB3.Client
dotnet add package DotNetEnv
```

## Code

```csharp
using System.Net;
using InfluxDB3.Client;
using InfluxDB3.Client.Write;
using DotNetEnv;

const int TotalPoints    = 1_000_000;
const int BatchSize      = 10_000;   // ≤10k lines / 10 MB per request
const int MaxConcurrency = 4;
const int MaxAttempts    = 5;
const int Hosts          = 1_000;    // 1,000 hosts × 1,000 seconds = 1M unique points

Env.Load();
static string Require(string name) =>
    Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(name) is { Length: > 0 } v
        ? v : throw new InvalidOperationException($"{name} is not set");

var database = Require("INFLUXDB_DATABASE");
using var client = new InfluxDBClient(
    host: Require("INFLUXDB_HOST"),
    token: Require("INFLUXDB_TOKEN"),
    database: database);

// 1. Fail fast if the database doesn't exist. InfluxDB 3 silently auto-creates
//    databases on write, so a typo ("senor_data") would "succeed" into the wrong DB.
try
{
    await foreach (var _ in client.Query("SELECT 1")) break;
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    Console.Error.WriteLine($"Database '{database}' is not reachable or doesn't exist: {ex.Message}");
    Console.Error.WriteLine($"Create it: influxdb3 create database {database} --token \"$INFLUXDB_ADMIN_TOKEN\"");
    return 1;
}

// 2. Pick a fixed start time once, so a retried batch gets the same timestamps
//    and merges into the existing points instead of adding duplicates.
var start = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(-TotalPoints / Hosts);
start = new DateTime(start.Ticks - start.Ticks % TimeSpan.TicksPerSecond, DateTimeKind.Utc);

List<PointData> BuildBatch(int batchNo)
{
    var batch = new List<PointData>(BatchSize);
    int first = batchNo * BatchSize;
    for (int p = first; p < Math.Min(first + BatchSize, TotalPoints); p++)
    {
        batch.Add(PointData.Measurement("sensor")
            .SetTag("host", $"server{p % Hosts:D4}")
            .SetTag("region", "us-west")
            .SetField("temperature", 20 + Random.Shared.NextDouble() * 10)
            .SetField("humidity", 40 + Random.Shared.NextDouble() * 20)
            .SetTimestamp(start.AddSeconds(p / Hosts)));  // unique per (host, second)
    }
    return batch;
}

static bool IsRetryable(Exception ex, CancellationToken ct) => ex switch
{
    InfluxDBApiException api => api.StatusCode == HttpStatusCode.TooManyRequests
                                || (int)api.StatusCode >= 500,
    HttpRequestException      => true,                        // no response
    TaskCanceledException     => !ct.IsCancellationRequested, // client timeout
    _                         => false                        // 400/401/403/404/413: surface
};

async Task WriteWithRetry(List<PointData> batch, int batchNo, CancellationToken ct)
{
    for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++)
    {
        try
        {
            await client.WritePointsAsync(batch, precision: WritePrecision.S, cancellationToken: ct);
            return;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (attempt < MaxAttempts && IsRetryable(ex, ct))
        {
            // Exponential backoff with full jitter, capped at 30 s
            var capMs = Math.Min(30_000, 500 * Math.Pow(2, attempt - 1));
            var delay = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(Random.Shared.NextDouble() * capMs);
            Console.Error.WriteLine($"Batch {batchNo} attempt {attempt} failed ({ex.Message}); retrying in {delay.TotalMilliseconds:F0} ms");
            await Task.Delay(delay, ct);
        }
    }
}

// 3. Build each batch when it's needed and send up to MaxConcurrency at a time.
int batches = (TotalPoints + BatchSize - 1) / BatchSize;
int done = 0;
var sw = System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch.StartNew();

await Parallel.ForEachAsync(
    Enumerable.Range(0, batches),
    new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = MaxConcurrency },
    async (batchNo, ct) =>
    {
        await WriteWithRetry(BuildBatch(batchNo), batchNo, ct);
        var n = Interlocked.Increment(ref done);
        if (n % 10 == 0) Console.WriteLine($"{n}/{batches} batches written");
    });

Console.WriteLine($"Wrote {TotalPoints:N0} points in {sw.Elapsed.TotalSeconds:F1}s");
return 0;
```

## Why it's built this way

- **Batches of 10,000 sent in parallel.** One request per point would mean 1M HTTP round trips. This sends 100 requests, 4 at a time. The C# client has no automatic batching like the Python client's `batch_size`, so the code does the batching itself.
- **Each point has its own timestamp, in seconds.** Points with the same table, tag set and timestamp merge, and there's no guarantee which field value survives. Each `(host, second)` pair here is unique, so nothing merges by accident. If one series can have more than one point per second, use `WritePrecision.Ms` or finer.
- **Some errors are retried and some stop the run:**
  - 429, 5xx and no response are retried with backoff and jitter.
  - 400, 401, 403, 404 and 413 are thrown right away, which stops the run so you see them.
  - Retrying is still not fully safe: because the timestamps are fixed, a retried batch merges into the points already written instead of duplicating them, but it doesn't reliably replace them.
- **The client writes through `/api/v2/write` by default (1.9.0+).** On that endpoint, one bad line rejects the whole batch: fix it and resend the whole batch. If you want partial writes (valid lines are kept and the 400 lists the rejected ones) or `NoSync`, set the `UseV2Api` write option to `false` to use `/api/v3/write_lp`. Check the [client README](https://github.com/InfluxCommunity/influxdb3-csharp) for the exact syntax in your version.
- **Untrusted input:** the data here is synthetic. If tag or field values come from users, remove `\n` and `\r` before writing. Line protocol can't escape them, so a newline would create a forged extra point.

